## Reusable knowledge

- The original `ReadingList.md` enumerated 14 readings: OpenVLA; π0, π0.5, π*0.6; ω-0; BitVLA; FP8; Microscaling/MX; NV
FP4; SmoothQuant; AWQ; SpinQuant; HAQ; TurboQuant. User-promoted Outlier Suppression #16 and Flow Matching #17 were fol
lowed by Self-Flow core #18; OCP OFP8 and MX remain companion normative specifications. Progression: VLA foundations/sy
stems -> Physical Intelligence series -> low-precision formats -> quantization algorithms -> hardware-aware deployment.
 [Task 1][Task 9][Task 10][Task 12]
- `π₀.₆-MEM` is separate from `π*₀.₆`: π*₀.₆ uses RECAP/advantage-conditioned post-training to learn from experience; M
EM adds multi-scale context/memory. MEM uses dense short-term video/proprioception for details and compressed long-term
 text for semantic state; high-level policy predicts subtask and language-memory update, low-level policy emits action 
chunks. [Task 2]
- “Up to 15 minutes” primarily concerns compressed text memory; dense observation history reaches 18 frames/54 seconds.
 In-context adaptation is context-conditioned strategy switching, not online gradients. No official MEM code, weights/c
heckpoint, or dataset link was verified as of 2026-08-25. Keep MEM as a PI-series companion after Week 2: `π₀.₅ = gener
alize → π*₀.₆ = improve from experience → MEM = remember context`; do not inflate the core count. [Task 2]
- Final post-MEM validation: 25 PDFs readable and unencrypted, 35 Markdown files, 23 paper READMEs, zero broken local l
inks, zero actual delivery placeholders. Workspace is not Git; use direct file validation. Per-paper notes use Backgrou
nd, Problem, Method, Key Innovation, Main Results, Limitations, Why It Matters, and Reading Questions. [Task 1][Task 2]
- Yawei Li alignment is efficient AI systems for LLMs/VLMs/VLAs: compression, quantization/pruning, memory/latency redu
ction, edge deployment, and software-hardware co-design. Route: RT-1/RT-2 -> OpenVLA/SmolVLA -> UniPi, GR-1/GR-2, UWM -
> WorldVLA, DreamZero, GigaWorld-Policy, WAM survey -> Li-aligned efficiency papers. [Task 3]
- `QuaRot: Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs` is NeurIPS 2024 Main Conference Track / arXiv `2404.00456` v2.
 Use the conceptual bridge `QuaRot: function-preserving fixed/randomized Hadamard rotation → SpinQuant: learned mergeab
le rotations optimized under quantized loss`. Local artifacts are `reading-guide/papers/12a-quarot/paper.pdf` and `READ
ME.md`. [Task 4]
- QuaRot applies randomized Hadamard rotations across residual, FFN, attention, and KV-cache paths before low-bit quant
ization. Its strong default 4-bit weight path uses GPTQ calibration (`128` WikiText-2 samples × sequence length `2048`)
; “calibration-free” mainly describes the RTN 6/8-bit path. Keep it as a companion: after integration, the guide report
s 15 core papers, 9 companions, 2 specifications, and 26 local PDFs. [Task 4]
- Keep the two controversy claim sets separate: Gao et al., `Revisiting RaBitQ and TurboQuant: A Symmetric Comparison o
f Methods, Theory, and Experiments` (arXiv `2604.19528v2`), reports asymmetric RaBitQ baseline conditions, worse TurboQ
uant results in most tested comparable settings, and unreproducible runtime/recall figures; the separate DRIVE/EDEN not
e (arXiv `2604.18555v1`) argues `TurboQuant_mse` is EDEN with fixed `S=1`. Neither is an independent misconduct investi
gation. [Task 5]
- Canonical TurboQuant is the ICLR 2026 proceedings final. Retain arXiv v1 only for version-drift/controversy audit and
 label it: final LongBench 2.5-bit average `49.74` differs from arXiv v1 `49.44`. [Task 5]
- Original RaBitQ is SIGMOD 2024, DOI `10.1145/3654970`, arXiv `2405.12497v1`; the multi-bit extension is SIGMOD 2025, 
DOI `10.1145/3725413`, arXiv `2409.09913v1`. Current code is `VectorDB-NTU/RaBitQ-Library`; the original and extension 
repos are archived separately. [Task 6]
- RaBitQ is mathematically architecture-agnostic but does not automatically accelerate arbitrary Transformer inference:
 require direct compressed-code kernels, amortized rotations, counted metadata, and matched-hardware benchmarks. `K` is
 a natural corrected-inner-product target; `V` generally needs reconstruction/weighted-sum treatment. `RaBitQCache` is 
precedent for attention-score proxy/sparse retrieval, not complete K/V reconstruction. AQLM, QuIP#, HIGGS, and VPTQ mea
n novelty needs a stronger estimator, guarantee, kernel, or deployment result. [Task 6]
- Final RaBitQ integration validation: 31 PDFs / 194,573,087 bytes, zero broken local Markdown links, structurally read
able PDFs; this workspace is not Git, so inspect files directly. [Task 6]
- Canonical FlashVLA identity is `FlashVLA: Streaming Action Decoding for Fast and Asynchronous VLA Inference`, arXiv `
2608.27384v1` (2026-08-27). It uses a staggered buffer of action chunks at different noise levels, advances every chunk
 per forward pass, emits one refined chunk after warm-up, and applies chunk-wise causal attention from cleaner/earlier 
chunks to noisier/later chunks. It is a VLA-systems companion following `π₀.₅`, not an addition to the 15 assigned core
 papers. [Task 7]
- FlashVLA local artifacts are `reading-guide/papers/03a-flashvla/README.md` and `paper-arxiv-v1.pdf`; the self-reading
 README covers 20/75-minute routes, 14 unanswered Reading Questions, a meeting card, evidence boundary, and limitations
. The guide warns that `19.9×` is action-decoding profile speedup, not end-to-end robot speedup; `30 Hz` depends on asy
nchronous overlap, and policy latency differs from low-level control frequency. Final validation: 32 readable, unencryp
ted PDFs / `199448119` bytes / `PDF_BAD=0` / `BROKEN_LOCAL_LINKS=0`. [Task 7]
- Calibration determines scale, zero-point, and possibly clipping ranges from representative data. Static quantization 
fixes them before inference; dynamic quantization computes activation parameters from current input. `Using activations
 during calibration ≠ quantizing activations during inference.` Static weight-only deployment can store INT4/INT8 weigh
ts while activations stay FP16/BF16; QAT simulates quantization/dequantization during training or fine-tuning, unlike P
TQ. QLoRA normally freezes quantized base weights and trains LoRA parameters, so it is not full QAT. [Task 8]
- `Outlier Suppression: Pushing the Limit of Low-bit Transformer Language Models` is Wei et al., NeurIPS 2022, DOI `10.
52202/068431-1265`, arXiv `2209.13325`. Gamma Migration function-preservingly moves LayerNorm γ into following branches
/weights; Token-Wise Clipping uses coarse token-extrema search then fine optimization. Its BERT/RoBERTa/BART PTQ/QAT ev
idence is not SmoothQuant evidence and does not establish modern LLM/VLA deployment success. Artifacts: `reading-guide/
papers/16-outlier-suppression/`. [Task 9]
- `π₀: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control` is the defensible first explicitly claimed Flow M
atching VLA with high-frequency action chunks; `AdaFlow` and `Riemannian Flow Matching Policy` are earlier robot-policy
 work. The foundation is Lipman et al., `Flow Matching for Generative Modeling`, ICLR 2023 / arXiv `2210.02747`, not a 
robotics paper. Its standalone core #17 is `reading-guide/papers/17-flow-matching/`; “simulation-free” describes traini
ng, not ODE sampling, and straight conditional OT pairs do not ensure a globally straight/optimal marginal flow or one-
step inference. [Task 10]
- Broad “add REPA-style representation alignment to VLA” is closed by verified near-neighbors: FLARE aligns future-toke
n states in a flow-matching action DiT; Spatial Forcing aligns VLA visual tokens to frozen VGGT; VEGA aligns OpenVLA-OF
T visual tokens to DINOv2-FiT3D; AGRA repurposes alignment in WAM; Robust-WAM aligns future action-stream queries to fu
ture DINOv3 CLS targets. The search-bounded opening is exact iREPA: no checked robot-policy paper implemented both `3×3
 Conv projector` and spatial normalization of teacher patch tokens in a matched closed-loop VLA study. [Task 11]
- For an Action-iREPA study, compare Base, REPA-style MLP, Conv-only, spatial-normalization-only, exact iREPA-style, gl
obal future-CLS, and 3D-patch-teacher baselines while holding backbone, data, action horizon, noise schedule, optimizer
, hardware, controller, and evaluator fixed. Report closed-loop success/progress, learning curves, steps/wall-clock to 
threshold, teacher overhead, training/inference memory, P50/P95/P99 latency, control frequency, power/energy, and OOD r
obustness; REPA's `17.5×` training-step claim is not wall-clock or robot-control speedup. [Task 11]
- Self-Flow is Hila Chefer et al., `Self-Supervised Flow Matching for Scalable Multi-Modal Synthesis`, ICML 2026 accept
ed poster `65011`, OpenReview `HoThWhfxiK`, arXiv `2603.06507v1`. The canonical local artifact is `reading-guide/papers
/18-self-flow/paper-arxiv-v1.pdf` (37 pages), explicitly an arXiv author manuscript rather than an ICML proceedings bin
ary. It emphasizes `Flow Matching loss`, `Dual-Timestep Scheduling`, a cleaner-view internal `EMA teacher`, joint repre
sentation/generation training, multimodal results, and limited `SIMPLER` video-action transfer. [Task 12]
- `From SRA to Self-Flow: Data Augmentation or Self-Supervision?` (`arXiv:2607.02508v1`) is a separate 10-page critical
 companion, not a renamed/revised Self-Flow. Final integration validated 51 PDFs, 49 Markdown files, 277 local links wi
th zero broken links, and matching inventory rows/files/page counts/sizes/SHA-256. [Task 12]
- New core entries are #20 HBVLA (`2602.13710v2`), #21 `WorldCache: Content-Aware Caching for Accelerated Video World M
odels` (`2603.22286v1`), and #22 `WorldCache: Accelerating World Models for Free via Heterogeneous Token Caching` (`260
3.06331v2`). Artifacts are `reading-guide/papers/20-hbvla/`, `21-worldcache-content-aware/`, and `22-worldcache-heterog
eneous-token-caching/`; validation found 54 PDFs, 53 Markdown files, 304 local links, and no broken links/placeholders.
 [Task 13]
- HBVLA uses action-aware rectified Hessian weighting, salient/non-salient partitioning, and Haar-domain 1-bit PTQ; its
 missing appendix and unsupported `2.93×` protocol are reproducibility warnings. Content-Aware WorldCache (`CFC`, `SWD`
, `OFA`, `ATS`) and Heterogeneous Token WorldCache (`CHTP`, `CAS`) measure video/world-model throughput/fidelity under 
different hardware/model settings, not closed-loop policy success; do not compare their speedups directly. [Task 13]

