D:\Downloads\Final Year Project\ideaspark_run\world-model-quantization\phase1\phase1_output.json:30:  "bottleneck_statement": "For any action-conditioned latent world model whose quantized transition is iterated inside a planner, low-bit PTQ has a structural mismatch between local numerical fidelity and multi-step decision fidelity: a perturbation that is small under layer or calibration statistics can be repeatedly fed into the latent transition and change the ordering of imagined action sequences, so the planning objective no longer reliably tracks task success as the horizon and visited states change. arxiv:2602.02110v1 demonstrates this primary instance in DINO-WM: weight-only and joint weight-activation PTQ behave differently by module, granularity, and horizon, and aggressive settings break planning-objective/task-success alignment, but the study remains a two-task visual-planning characterization. arxiv:2607.28405v1 shows the neighboring WAM setting is also rollout- and deployment-distribution-sensitive, yet its joint future/action denoising setting does not establish the corresponding failure for latent-dynamics imagined rollouts. The gap is therefore a missing control-relevant account of when finite-precision numerical error becomes a planning-decision error, not the absence of a particular quantizer.",
